KPDL — LLM-Language Edition, Version 1.1 (KPDL-1.1-LLM-Language)
Copyright (c) 2026 Marc-Dennis Haberland

Preamble. LLM-Language is an independent programming language (specification,
compilers, reference implementation, tools, documentation).
This license separates three things: (a) the rights in LLM-Language as a
language and technology remain with the original rights holder; (b)
developers may use and extend LLM-Language and build proprietary applications
on it, provided LLM-Language and the rights holder are expressly credited on
use and distribution and the LLM-Language base is never presented as someone
else's original work; (c) companies with annual revenue of EUR 3 million
or more need a paid Enterprise License from the rights holder (Section
10), while research institutions use LLM-Language free of charge (Section 11).

1. Definitions. "Rights Holder" means Marc-Dennis Haberland as the sole
rights holder of LLM-Language for this license. Rights under this license
are granted by him; any consent required under this license is his alone.
"LLM-Language" means the language in all its parts (specification,
intermediate representations, reference implementation in source and
binary form, compilers, interpreters, tools, test suites, documentation).
"Protected Components" are all parts of LLM-Language. "Derivative Work" is any
implementation, extension, modification, translation, port or other
derived version based on the LLM-Language specification, reference
implementation or Protected Components, regardless of language or
platform. "Work Based on LLM-Language" is any software or product that uses,
embeds, compiles or executes LLM-Language or contains programs written in
LLM-Language without modifying LLM-Language itself. "Distribution" is any
publication, provision (including as a network service) or transfer to
third parties, in source or binary form, for a fee or free of charge.
"Company" is any legal entity or partnership engaged in commercial or
business activity, including all affiliated entities of its corporate
group. "Annual Revenue" is the consolidated total revenue of the
corporate group in the last completed fiscal year, in EUR or the
equivalent at the annual average exchange rate. "Research Institution"
is a university, public or non-profit research organisation or research
institute, including its institutes, chairs, working groups and
affiliated institutes; R&D departments of companies are not Research
Institutions under this license.

2. Reservation of Rights. All rights in LLM-Language as a language and
technology, including copyright in the specification and reference
implementation and the right in the name "LLM-Language" for this language,
remain with the Rights Holder. This license transfers no rights in the
LLM-Language language; it only grants the permissions in Section 3. No
Derivative Work or Work Based on LLM-Language may present the LLM-Language base as
its own or a third party's original, or suggest that LLM-Language originates
from anyone other than the Rights Holder.

3. Grant. Subject to this license, the Rights Holder grants you a
worldwide, non-exclusive right to (3.1) use, copy and run LLM-Language,
privately and commercially; (3.2) modify, extend and port LLM-Language and
create Derivative Works; (3.3) create Works Based on LLM-Language, including
proprietary ones under any license of your choice, and distribute them;
(3.4) distribute Derivative Works provided Sections 4 and 5 are met.
(3.5) These rights are royalty-free for natural persons, Research
Institutions (Section 11) and Companies below the revenue threshold in
Section 10; Companies at or above that threshold exercise the rights in
3.1 to 3.4 only under a valid Enterprise License (Section 10).

4. Attribution. Any published or distributed implementation, extension,
modification or derived version based on the LLM-Language specification, the
LLM-Language reference implementation or Protected Components of LLM-Language must
identify LLM-Language as its basis and name the original developer and Rights Holder (Marc-Dennis Haberland). Any distributed
Work Based on LLM-Language must recognizably name LLM-Language as the underlying
programming language and name the Rights Holder. The notice must appear
where recipients customarily find it (license/NOTICE file, about dialog,
documentation, product page), with at least:

    This product uses the LLM-Language programming language.

    LLM-Language
    Copyright (c) 2026 Marc-Dennis Haberland
    Licensed under the KPDL — LLM-Language Edition (KPDL-1.1-LLM-Language).

    LLM-Language is an independent programming language. This software is a
    work based on LLM-Language and does not transfer any rights in the LLM-Language
    language.

Existing copyright, license and notice statements in Protected
Components may not be removed or altered; a copy of this license must
accompany Derivative Works.

5. Name and Identification. The name "LLM-Language" must be preserved as the
designation of the language on use and distribution. Derivative Works
may carry their own names but must credit LLM-Language under Section 4.
Neither "LLM-Language" nor the Rights Holder's name may be used to suggest
that Derivative Works or Works Based on LLM-Language originate from, were
reviewed by or are endorsed by the Rights Holder without express written
consent. A version deviating from the published LLM-Language specification may
not be called "LLM-Language" without a distinguishing addition where confusion
with the original is possible.

6. Your Own Rights. Rights in your own extensions, programs,
applications and tools remain yours; this license requires neither
disclosure of your source code nor distribution under this license. The
LLM-Language base itself remains subject to this license (Sections 2, 4, 5)
whenever you distribute it, modified or unmodified.

7. Contributions. Contributors to the reference implementation or
specification grant the Rights Holder a non-exclusive, irrevocable,
worldwide, royalty-free right to use their contribution as part of
LLM-Language under this or later versions of this license; contributors keep
their own rights in their contribution.

8. No Warranty; Limitation of Liability. LLM-Language is provided "as is",
without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including
merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose and non-infringement.
To the extent permitted by law, the Rights Holder is not liable for
any damages arising from the use of or inability to use LLM-Language.
Mandatory statutory liability (in particular for intent, gross
negligence and injury to life, body or health) remains unaffected. This
also applies to Enterprise Licenses under Section 10.

9. Termination. Your rights terminate automatically upon breach of
Sections 2, 4, 5 or 10; they are reinstated if the breach is fully cured
within 30 days of becoming aware of it (for Section 10, by purchasing
the Enterprise License due, including the period of use since the end
of the evaluation period), unless the Rights Holder has terminated the
license in writing beforehand. Rights lawfully obtained by third parties
before termination survive as long as those parties comply with this
license.

10. Enterprise License from EUR 3 million Annual Revenue. (10.1) A
Company with Annual Revenue of EUR 3,000,000 or more requires a paid
Enterprise License from the Rights Holder for any use of LLM-Language under
Section 3 (development, operation, embedding in products, provision as
a service). The threshold applies to the corporate group; splitting use
across subsidiaries to stay below it is disregarded. (10.2) The
Enterprise License is granted per using legal entity and covers all its
employees, sites and products without limit on developers or
installations; a group license for several entities may be agreed in
writing. (10.3) Fees (net, plus applicable VAT, as of 20 September
2026): Annual Revenue from EUR 3,000,000 to below EUR 50,000,000 —
EUR 49 per month or EUR 490 per year; EUR 50,000,000 and above — EUR 199
per month or EUR 1,990 per year. The Rights Holder may adjust fees for
new license periods; running periods keep the agreed fee. Annual
licenses do not renew automatically. (10.4) Evaluation: such a Company
may evaluate and test LLM-Language free of charge for 60 days from first use,
provided no Work Based on LLM-Language is operated productively or distributed
during that time; afterwards an Enterprise License is required. (10.5)
Contributions under Section 7, merely reading the specification and
running the test suite to verify contributions require no Enterprise
License. (10.6) The Enterprise License does not alter Sections 2, 4, 5
and 6; attribution and reservation of rights remain. (10.7) License
requests go to the Rights Holder (contact: see the repository README);
the Enterprise License is concluded upon the Rights Holder's written
confirmation and payment of the fee.

11. Research Institutions. (11.1) Research Institutions may use LLM-Language
under Section 3 free of charge regardless of revenue or budget — for
research, teaching, publications and prototypes — provided they are in
principle open to a research partnership with the Rights Holder. It is
sufficient to be willing to talk about cooperation upon request (e.g.
joint evaluation of results, exchange of experience, participation in
the specification); there is no obligation to conclude a partnership.
(11.2) Publications based on LLM-Language credit LLM-Language and the Rights Holder
under Section 4 (a citation or acknowledgement suffices). (11.3) If
LLM-Language is transferred from a Research Institution into a product of a
Company under 10.1 (spin-off, licensing, contract research with
productive use at the client), Section 10 applies to that Company.

12. Miscellaneous. The Rights Holder may publish new versions of this
license; components published under "KPDL-1.1-LLM-Language" remain usable under this
version. If any provision is invalid, the remainder stays in force. The
laws of the Federal Republic of Germany apply, excluding the UN
Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods; venue, where
permissible, is the seat of the Rights Holder.
